UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same idea to a new game or situation, then compare two choices for safety and inclusion.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nWrite two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.\n\nWATCH FOR\nBelieving a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nMake a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and two food safety steps. Present it to a family member and collect two stars and a wish.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one choice you changed and why. Any movement is optional, adult-supervised and can be done in a gentler way or described instead.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"PDHPE","learning_intention":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Practise and apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Clear, safe movement space with an adult nearby for any physical activity","Optional: soft ball, cushion or water bottle"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["PH2-IHW-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Make a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and two food safety steps. Present it to a family member and collect two sta Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Original response on new material: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The… Reasoning: I used a showcase by attending to a balanced meal rather than copying the worked model.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Common error: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Correction: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a showcase.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail. / Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,… This matches the lesson skill for a showcase.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,…","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included."],"question":"Write two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. That misconception weakens accuracy; the other choices align with the lesson idea.","options":["A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to…","Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,…","Careful use of a balanced meal with clear evidence.","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included."],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when practising a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and named a balanced meal, not by copying the model.","options":["Choose randomly because any option could be right.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter.","Apply the idea with new evidence and name the a balanced meal used.","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence."],"question":"A learner is practising a showcase. Which approach best shows understanding?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,… uses safe, healthy, respectful choices appropriately for this lesson.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Copy the example and call that independent work.","Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,…","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included."],"question":"Which response best shows careful thinking about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows.","options":["Name a specific source, cue or example and say what it shows about a showcase.","Make a bigger claim without naming any source or cue.","Copy a classmate’s sentence with no local detail.","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included."],"question":"Which habit strengthens evidence about a balanced meal?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea with concrete detail.","options":["Ignore the safety rule because the first idea looked exciting.","Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk…","Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","Treat a skipped permission or safety step as if it did not matter."],"question":"Using this lesson idea — A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods,… — which choice is best?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Respect and accuracy mean checking evidence and honouring limits/protocols.","options":["Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete.","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster.","Treat every online claim as equally reliable.","Check facts/cues, acknowledge limits, and avoid inventing details."],"question":"Which action shows respect and accuracy together?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the skill.","options":["Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included.","Make a showcase about healthy eating for a younger child. Include the five groups, a balanced plate, one sometimes food rule, why water matters and…","Retype the worked example with no new example.","Copy the example and call that independent work."],"question":"Which task best shows independent use of a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Require independently produced work with a specific supporting detail.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating a showcase on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Original response on new material: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The… Reasoning: I used a showcase by attending to a balanced meal rather than copying the worked model.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Require misconception + correction + purpose-linked reason.","question":"Name one likely error with a showcase, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Common error: Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Correction: Sample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt,… Why: the correction restores accurate use of a showcase.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested. Share only what you feel comfortable sharing.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-pdhpe-y3-w15-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA PDHPE alignment using catalogue outcome codes (PH2-*); content-point mapping remains pending human review. Physical activities are optional, adult-supervised and adaptable; no child is required to share personal details.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise a showcase.","Read the mission before you start moving or deciding.","Keep your own safety and everyone''s inclusion in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reasons with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not keep everyone safe and included."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the cue or rule that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore safety, consent or inclusion.","Prefer a reasoned, respectful choice over one that merely looks fun."],"submit":["Confirm that your reasons and reflection are both present.","Proofread names, steps and safety words.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked example.","Name one exact cue, rule or choice before you decide.","Think through the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the example and list the key cues.","Say what each cue does for safety, health or friendship.","A final label alone is weaker than a stated reason."],"teach2":["Compare an unsafe or unkind choice with a better one.","Change the choice or strategy, not only the words.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh place, game or situation that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked example as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original reasons and a short reflection.","Save work that shows the skill in a new situation."]},"subject":"Healthy, Safe and Active Lifestyles","success_criteria":["I can use a showcase with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify a balanced meal and explain why it matters.","I can practise reasons independently on a new prompt.","I can notice respect for differences needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different…","options":["A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nWrite two sentences for a showcase saying why a mixed lunch is healthier than a lunch of chips and soft drink. Name food groups in your reasons.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","explain":"A healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Understand and demonstrate","visual":[{"alt":"A balanced wrap.","caption":"Four groups in one meal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wrap</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain, protein, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">water</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, c Then write your own reasons.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which cue or rule matters most.\nJuno: Name the cues, rules or choices that keep everyone safe and included before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, c Then write your own reasons."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice. Look at each cue, rule or choice that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key cues, rules or choices from the example and say what each one does for safety, health or relationships.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful reasoning names the cue, rule or strategy and the reason it helps, not only a label such as \"good\".","explain":"Make the PDHPE idea visible: name the body cue, safety rule or wellbeing strategy you used and say why it helps you or others.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without a reason does not show PDHPE thinking. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Show the reasoning","visual":[{"alt":"Respect for food differences.","caption":"Respect every table.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">different foods and budgets</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Allergy</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">plan for safe foods</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason.","Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBelieving a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Here is a more helpful example: Example: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against choice does not work, then write a safer, kinder or healthier choice with a reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nSample: ''A mixed lunch with a sandwich, salad, fruit and water uses four food groups, which give different nutrients. Chips and soft drink mostly give salt, fat and sugar and no variety.'' The reasons name groups and nutrients.","explain":"Use an intentionally unsafe or unhelpful choice as a comparison. Name the specific PDHPE error, then show the safer or more respectful choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without changing the choice does not fix the error. Believing a healthy diet means giving up the foods you love. A healthy diet is about balance and variety, with enjoyment included. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion. Supported choices follow the actual cues, rules and purpose of the task.","options":["Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.","A choice unrelated to safety, health or respect.","An answer copied without understanding.","A guess with no reason."],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about a showcase?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nA healthy food showcase brings together the five food groups, balanced plates, everyday and sometimes foods, water and food safety. Choose a clear way to present, such as a poster, a recipe card or a short talk, and use real examples with reasons. Be respectful of different foods, cultures, budgets and allergies when talking about meals. Now choose a different situation to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe or practise one fresh situation that uses the same skill and write a short reason for your choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new situation should use the same PDHPE idea as the example, but happen in a different place or with different people.","explain":"Use the same PDHPE idea in a new place, game or situation, not a copy of the example. Review safety, fairness and inclusion.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make safe, healthy and respectful choices using clear cues and reasons, rather than guessing or copying others. Every choice keeps bodies safe and includes everyone.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the cues, rules or choices that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. For any movement, use a clear space, ask an adult to be nearby, move at your own comfortable level and stop if anything hurts or feels unsafe. You may always choose a gentler version or only describe the movement. One deliberate, reasoned change is more useful than a longer answer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to a new situation. Change the choice or strategy, not just the wording.","title":"Transfer to a new situation"}],"title":"Year 3, Week 15, Lesson 2: Unit review and showcase","unit_number":3,"unit_title":"Healthy food and nutrition","week":15,"week_in_unit":5,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nExample: Leo''s recipe card ''Rainbow wraps''. Food groups: grain (wrap), protein (chicken), vegetables (carrot, lettuce, capsicum), milk (cheese). Safety: wash hands and use separate boards. Drink: water. Sometimes food: a small cookie on Fridays. He gives a reason for each choice.\n\nWrite what makes this example safe, healthy or respectful, naming the cues, rules or choices used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"A balanced wrap.","caption":"Four groups in one meal.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Wrap</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">grain, protein, vegetables, milk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Drink</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">water</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"Respect for food differences.","caption":"Respect every table.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Respect</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">different foods and budgets</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Allergy